// @run
// @log a
// @log null
// @log a
// @log null
// @log b
// CONTRACT: `print(v)` over an UN-NARROWED `K | null` niche prints the MEMBER, or the word `null`
// when the value is null — the same rendering the two nullable scalars beside it give
// (`print(<string | null>)` and `print(<boolean | null>)`), at the param, local, global and
// return positions.
//
// It is not a reject, and the earlier ruling that it should be rested on a false parity: the
// stated rule was "the same rule the nullable STRING beside it carries: narrow it first", but the
// nullable string does NOT require narrowing — it prints `null`. The niche's member ids and its
// `-1` sentinel PARTITION the i32, so the value carries its own discriminator and there is
// nothing to infer; printing a list of nullable enum values is ordinary.
//
// Master emitted an EMPTY widening chain here — the member texts came from a query that
// deliberately declines a nullable type — so this program built and printed NOTHING: rc=0, no
// output, the quietest possible wrong answer. The chain now takes its texts from the nullable
// type and appends the sentinel arm.
//
// The NARROWED spelling is `print-narrowed-nullable-litunion.vl`, which has always run.

type K = "a" | "b"

function show(v: K | null) { print(v) }

show("a")
show(null)

const g: K | null = "a"
print(g)

const h: K | null = null
print(h)

function mk(): K | null { return "b" }

print(mk())
